@page "/collections"

<CatHead Title="Collections">
    Four ways to show a set of things. Pick by what the reader does with it: compare values
    (a <a href="/table">table</a>), open one at a time (<code>.list</code>), follow an order
    (<code>.steps</code>), or expand into detail (<code>.accordion</code>, <code>.tree</code>).
    A record of what happened, with when, is a <a href="/timeline">timeline</a>.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Collections.List" Title="List" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        For rows where a table would be wrong: when the fields differ per row, when one field
        dominates and the rest supports it, or when each row is something you <em>open</em> rather
        than compare down a column.
    </p>
    <p>
        A row that navigates is an <code>&lt;a class="list-row"&gt;</code> filling the
        <code>&lt;li&gt;</code>, never a click handler on the <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> — the whole
        row is then a real link, openable in a new tab and reachable by keyboard.
        <code>.list-main</code> carries <code>min-width:0</code>, which is what makes the ellipsis
        work: a flex item otherwise refuses to shrink below its content, and truncation silently
        never happens.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>A row that acts rather than navigates is a
        <code>&lt;button class="list-row"&gt;</code></strong>, filling the <code>&lt;li&gt;</code>
        the same way. The class resets the platform's button chrome — the system fill, the system
        border, the centred text and the UA's own font, none of which a <code>&lt;span&gt;</code>
        row brings — so the two rows look alike, and hover, pressed, focus and disabled apply to
        both. A non-interactive row stays a <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> and gets none of those
        states. The <a href="/drawer">action sheet</a> is a list of button rows.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Collections.Steps" Title="Steps" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Progress through a sequence that has an order and an end — a wizard, an approval chain.
        State is <code>data-state</code> on the <code>&lt;li&gt;</code> rather than a modifier class,
        because a step has exactly one of three states and an attribute cannot accidentally carry
        two. The number comes from a CSS counter, so inserting a step is not a renumbering edit.
    </p>
    <p>
        Done is <strong>filled</strong> and pending is <strong>outlined</strong>. That difference
        survives the colour-blind palette, a greyscale print and forced colours — which a
        green-versus-grey fill would not — so no markup has to carry a tick.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Collections.StickyGroups" Title="Stacking group headers" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        A grouped list in a scroll container where each header pins <strong>below</strong> the one
        above it instead of replacing it, so the reader keeps the whole path to where they are.
        Scroll the card.
    </p>
    <p>
        The app supplies one number and nothing else:
        <code>style="--group-head-index: 2"</code>. The offset is computed from it, so no app does
        the arithmetic; the height is a variable so the two agree by construction; the background is
        opaque because rows scroll under it; <code>flex-shrink: 0</code> stops a flex scroll
        container squashing a pinned header to nothing; and the height in the multiplication is one
        pixel short, which overlaps each header on the one above so no sub-pixel sliver of a
        scrolling row shows between them. Every one of those was a bug first.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>An opaque header has to be the colour of what it is pinned over</strong>, so it is
        <code>--group-head-bg</code>, which reads <code>--surface-here</code> — the colour
        every library surface declares for its own contents — and falls back to the page. A
        list in a card, a modal, a drawer or a popover is right with no help. On a surface
        <em>your</em> app paints, set the variable on the list —
        <code>style="--group-head-bg: var(--surface-strong)"</code> — rather than restating the
        rule. <code>--group-head-fg</code> and <code>--group-head-height</code> are the same escape
        hatch for the label colour and the pinned height.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Collections.StickyGroupsRule" Title="Group headers as a caption" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>--rule</code> trades the full-width bottom border for a hairline running from the
        label to the end of the row, so the header reads as a caption over the rows rather than a
        bar across them. Use it where the groups are a state the reader scans past, not a section
        they land in. The pinning is identical.
    </p>
    <p>
        The rule is the header's own <code>::after</code>, which is why a trailing count needs
        <code>.sticky-group-count</code>: a pseudo-element is always the last child, and
        <code>order</code> is what puts the rule between the label and the count. The count sits at
        the end without the rule too.
    </p>
    <p>
        This list is on the page rather than in a card, and its headers take the page colour with no
        markup saying so.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Collections.Accordion" Title="Accordion" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Built on <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>/<code>&lt;summary&gt;</code>, which is the point: the
        open state, the keyboard operation and the announcement all come from the platform, so
        there is no ARIA to get wrong and nothing to wire.
    </p>
    <p>
        Give every <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> the same <code>name</code> to make them mutually
        exclusive — that is a platform feature too, and it needs no JavaScript. Try it: opening one
        below closes the others.
    </p>
    <p>
        <strong>If code has to open one, bind <code>open</code> one way and never write it back.</strong>
        <code>toggle</code> fires whenever the <code>open</code> attribute changes — including when
        the framework's own diff sets it. So a handler that mirrors the state back into a field flips
        the field the moment code opens the section, the next diff removes the attribute,
        <code>toggle</code> fires again, and the two fight. It is not a race: it is deterministic, and
        it shows up only once something other than a click opens the disclosure.
    </p>
    <p>
        Keep the summary's label independent of the open state as well. A label that flips between
        “Show” and “Hide” is what tempts you into reading the state back in the first place, and the
        caret already says which way it is.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Collections.Tree" Title="Tree" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        Nested structure the reader expands — a group hierarchy, a folder of files. Nested
        <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> inside <code>&lt;details&gt;</code>, for the same reason as the
        accordion.
    </p>
    <p>
        Do <strong>not</strong> declare <code>role="tree"</code>. A tree promises single-tab-stop
        navigation with arrow keys through a flattened view, which this markup does not implement;
        <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> announces a set of disclosures, which is what it is.
        <code>.tree-leaf</code> is an item with nothing under it, indented to line up with its
        siblings' labels.
    </p>
</CatExample>
